---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = "Omnisend vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify migration guide";
const desc = "Yotpo Email and SMS were retired. Evaluate Omnisend as a replacement while preserving review, loyalty, permission and suppression dependencies.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I choose Yotpo Email & SMS for a new program?",
    "answer": "Do not plan a new program on the retired products. Yotpo announced full Email/SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025, and its SMS lifecycle notice says dashboard and background services are no longer active."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does this mean Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty are retired?",
    "answer": "The Email/SMS retirement does not establish retirement of Reviews or Loyalty. Inventory their current role separately and confirm the supported integration for the replacement sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Omnisend an automatic replacement for every old flow?",
    "answer": "No. Yotpo named Omnisend as a migration partner, but that does not prove that your historical records, queued messages, loyalty events or sender identity transfer. Validate the exact replacement scope."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
<header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify decision guide</p>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">This is a replacement decision for a retired product, not a choice between two available email platforms. A merchant finding an old Yotpo comparison should first locate the store’s current sender and retained records, then evaluate Omnisend against the journeys that still need an owner.</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<p><em>Scope checked November 24, 2026. Supplier descriptions support scope only. Proposed exercises are not completed tests or observed merchant results.</em></p>
<h2>Retirement changes the question</h2>
<p>Yotpo’s <a class="link" href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration notice</a> scheduled full Email/SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025 and named Omnisend and Attentive as partners. Its <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">SMS lifecycle notice</a> now says the dashboard and background services are inactive. Those notices do not promise that an old account remains exportable or that any partner offer is still available. Ask support about retained records rather than assuming a migration wizard can retrieve them.</p>
<h2>Find the current system of record</h2>
<p>Identify where campaigns and lifecycle messages actually send today. Check Shopify app ownership, agency handover documents and retained exports. Record which evidence exists for subscription source, opt-out state, phone permission, templates and historical flow definitions. An old customer list alone cannot establish permission or reconstruct a delayed journey. If records are missing, document that recovery gap before activating an imported audience.</p>
<h2>Keep Reviews and Loyalty decisions separate</h2>
<p>Do not remove working review collection or points functionality merely because email moved. Inventory the messages those products still own: review invitations, points updates and promotional follow-ups may have different triggers. For each, record the sending system and exclusion rule. Confirm the supported integration and fields for the chosen replacement; belonging to the same vendor family is not proof that every event or suppression rule is shared.</p>
<h2>Where Omnisend belongs on the shortlist</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.omnisend.com/features/automation/">Omnisend’s automation overview</a> describes ecommerce workflows with email, SMS and web push. That scope makes it a candidate for replacing lifecycle messaging; it is not evidence that every historical Yotpo flow transfers. Ask for a mapping of the store’s welcome, checkout, post-purchase and winback requirements, including any review or loyalty dependency. Distinguish supported event exchange from an operator manually importing a segment.</p>
<h2>Proposed rehearsal: points change while a message waits</h2>
<p>This is an unperformed exercise. Use a test customer with a pending promotional message that refers to a loyalty reward. Redeem that reward before the message is due, then inspect whether the message updates, exits or retains stale content. Repeat with an order cancellation before a review invitation. If a timely event is unavailable, use content that does not assert a live balance and assign review requests to a single supported owner. Record the limitation rather than inventing parity.</p>
<h2>Rebuild journeys without replaying history</h2>
<p>Start welcome from a genuine new eligible signup, not the import date of every existing subscriber. For carts, avoid reviving obsolete checkouts from an archive. For post-purchase, reconcile review requests and product education so customers do not receive duplicate invitations. For winback, preserve meaningful purchase history where available rather than treating missing data as inactivity. Keep sending disabled until the expected entry and exit rules are observed with test profiles.</p>
<h2>Price the replacement, not an obsolete bundle</h2>
<p>Request current replacement pricing for the retained audience, normal and peak sends, required channels, support and integration work. Keep continuing Reviews or Loyalty charges separate from retired Email/SMS terms. Include recovery work and template rebuilding. An old bundle discount or historical starting price cannot establish the current cost of operating the replacement stack. No claimed merchant savings or recovery uplift is supported here.</p>
<h2>Release and recovery plan</h2>
<p>Assign one current sender per journey, reconcile suppressions and permission records, and start with a limited verified cohort. Confirm sender authentication, unsubscribe propagation and the supported path for any phone sender transfer before wider release. Retain recovered exports and the mapping from old journey intent to new configuration. If historical records cannot be recovered, keep that as an explicit operational limitation; do not claim a complete migration from a contact import alone.</p>
</article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-omnisend">Yotpo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">Reviews & UGC playbook</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/beauty">Email apps for beauty stores</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Yotpo Email & SMS FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
